finding: Bodies/shapes review of f7fe312a: sleeping body storage still does not take Box3D's reserve policy. crates/physics/src/solver_set.rs:495 (transfer_body_in_world) emplaces into the new sleeping body's array without reserving the island body count. BodyArray::emplace at :43-52 starts at 16 and doubles. The caller crates/physics/src/physics_world.rs:545-553 reserves only joint sims; there is no body reserve operation in SolverSet's sleep path. Box3D 47d7f7cc src/solver_set.c:187 reserves sleepSet->bodySims once to island->bodies.count before the transfer loop (contactIndices at :188 is likewise not reserved, although contacts are outside my allocated subsystem). This leaves stage 6's body-storage entry, “one reserve per destination,” incomplete, with no recorded language/platform binding for omitting the sleeping body reserve. The retained awake ECS-capacity bridge is not a reason for this sleeping-set path. For a 17-body connected island put to sleep into a fresh set, Shallot allocates 16 then 32 BodySim slots and copies the first 16 records during growth; native allocates exactly 17 once. Even a one-body island retains 16 slots instead of one. Values, step hashes, contact/awake/joint counts, and warmed steady allocation gates cannot detect that extra cold allocation/copy or the different retained capacity. Least red: a wasm-side SolverSet fixture with access to its private BodyArray capacity, sleeping one body into a fresh set and asserting capacity==1; alternatively a 17-body sleep fixture asserting capacity==17 and one destination allocation/no growth copy. Independently examined: the final diff in the allocated Rust and TypeScript files, body sim/state layouts and migration, shape offsets/geometry/lifecycle, identity databases and hull probing, explicit-world query/draw changes, snapshot prefixes, pertinent body/shape commit messages, and rewritten ccd-inertia, geometry-database and shape-materials tests. Compared storage/sleep/wake paths to native solver